%{ if try(parameters.common.internal_ca.enabled, false) ~}
---
# Internal mTLS CA — one private trust root PER ENVIRONMENT, for the fitmate-* namespaces.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (ADR-059): FITMate services must speak mTLS to each other. The two existing
# ClusterIssuers are both ACME (letsencrypt-dns01, -prod) and cannot serve internal service
# identity — Let's Encrypt will not issue for *.svc.cluster.local, and a public root is the wrong
# trust anchor for pod-to-pod anyway. The only private CA in the cluster is vault-ca-issuer, which
# is a NAMESPACED Issuer in the `vault` namespace, so fitmate-* cannot reference it.
#
#
# ── 🔴 CONSTRAINT 1: where a ClusterIssuer reads its CA secret from ──────────────────────────
#
# A ClusterIssuer of type `ca` does NOT resolve spec.ca.secretName from the namespace of the
# Certificate that references it. It resolves it from cert-manager's own "cluster resource
# namespace". Measured on this install 2026-08-28 — the controller runs with:
#
#     --cluster-resource-namespace=$(POD_NAMESPACE)      → resolves to `cert-manager`
#
# and secret `vault-tls-ca` exists ONLY in namespace `vault`. So the obvious move — a ClusterIssuer
# pointing at `vault-tls-ca` — would sit permanently NotReady with "secret not found". Promoting
# the CA is therefore NOT a one-line kind: Issuer -> kind: ClusterIssuer change. The CA material
# has to be reachable from `cert-manager`, and cert-manager does not sync Secrets across
# namespaces.
#
# A selfSigned ClusterIssuer needs NO secret at all. That breaks the chicken-and-egg cleanly: it
# mints a CA Certificate directly INTO `cert-manager`, where a `ca` ClusterIssuer can then read
# it. Zero secret copying, zero root key in Terraform state, no new runtime component.
#
# ⚠️ Nothing in the `vault` namespace is read, modified, or referenced by this file. Vault's
# serving path (vault-selfsigned -> vault-tls-ca -> vault-ca-issuer -> vault-tls-server) is
# untouched. That is a hard requirement — Vault is live and everything else authenticates to it.
# The internal mTLS roots are deliberately independent of Vault's CA: vault-tls-ca is a
# VAULT-SERVING CA, and conflating "may serve the secret store" with "is a FITMate service" builds
# exactly the over-broad trust root NIST SP 800-204B warns against.
#
#
# ── 🔴 CONSTRAINT 2: this is why each environment gets its OWN SELF-SIGNED ROOT ──────────────
#
# Namespaces are per-service-per-environment (fitmate-<service>-<env>), so a single CA serving all
# environments would make a dev-issued certificate chain correctly from prod's point of view.
#
# The first design here was one shared root with a per-environment INTERMEDIATE beneath it. That
# is the textbook shape, and on this cert-manager version it would have SILENTLY NOT WORKED.
#
# cert-manager sets a leaf Secret's `ca.crt` to the highest SELF-SIGNED certificate it can see,
# not to the immediate issuer — kube.SecretTLSKeyPairAndCA appends the issuer Secret's own
# `ca.crt` to the chain it signs with. Measured on this cluster, on the existing Vault chain,
# which has exactly that shape:
#
#     secret vault-tls-ca      keys = [ca.crt, tls.crt, tls.key]   ← issuer carries its own ca.crt
#     secret vault-tls-server  ca.crt subject = CN=vault-ca, issuer = CN=vault-ca (self-signed)
#
# So with a shared root, EVERY environment's leaf Secret would carry the SHARED ROOT in `ca.crt`.
# A service pinning its own `ca.crt` — the obvious and documented thing to do — would have trusted
# dev, stg and prod alike. The isolation would have looked correct in every manifest and existed
# nowhere: a control that does not exist, which is worse than a known gap.
#
# Per-environment SELF-SIGNED roots remove the failure mode instead of documenting around it.
# Each environment's leaf gets `ca.crt` = that environment's root, so the natural thing an
# application does is also the correct thing. A dev certificate cannot validate against prod's
# trust anchor — not by policy, but because no chain exists.
#
#     per environment <env>:
#       ClusterIssuer  <prefix>-<env>-selfsigned    (selfSigned — no secret needed; bootstrap)
#         └── Certificate  <prefix>-<env>           [cert-manager]  isCA, self-signed ROOT
#               └── ClusterIssuer  <prefix>-<env>   ← what fitmate-<service>-<env> references
#
# NAME CONSTRAINTS WERE CONSIDERED AND ARE NOT AVAILABLE HERE. spec.nameConstraints is in the
# installed CRD schema, but on cert-manager v1.16.1 `NameConstraints` is an ALPHA feature gate
# defaulting to FALSE in both the controller and the webhook, and this install passes no
# --feature-gates at all — the webhook would reject such a Certificate outright. (The v1.16 docs
# page claims Beta/default-true; it is serving v1.17 content. The gate was promoted in v1.17.0.)
# Worse, v1.16.x writes spec.nameConstraints.permitted.uriDomains into the EXCLUDED set — a permit
# rule silently becomes a deny rule — fixed in v1.17.4/v1.18.2 and never backported to
# release-1.16. Revisit only after upgrading past v1.17.4, and enable the gate on BOTH
# Deployments: turning it on for the webhook alone yields a silently unconstrained CA that still
# reports Ready.
#
#
# ── 🔴 usages: LEAF certificates must list client auth EXPLICITLY ────────────────────────────
#
# https://cert-manager.io/docs/usage/certificate/ says the default usages are "digital signature,
# key encipherment, and server auth". That page is WRONG and has been for five years — `serverAuth`
# was added for macOS Catalina in v0.12 (PR #2303), then REMOVED in v0.15.0 (PR #2864) because it
# broke gRPC mTLS (issue #2407). The v0.15.0 note is marked ACTION REQUIRED. The docs bug is
# cert-manager/website#706, filed 2021, closed unfixed 2025. The API reference is the accurate
# one: "If unset, defaults to `digital signature` and `key encipherment`."
#
# So the real default is worse than the ADR assumed: NEITHER server auth NOR client auth is set.
# Confirmed by measurement here — the existing vault-tls-server leaf sets no usages and was issued
# with NO Extended Key Usage extension at all.
#
# That happens to work: RFC 5280 §4.2.1.12 makes an absent EKU unrestricted, and Go's
# checkChainForKeyUsage skips EKU-less certs outright, so mTLS would succeed in both directions
# between two Go services. Do NOT build on that. It is Go-specific leniency, it stops applying the
# moment anything non-Go (Envoy, JSSE, nginx, OpenSSL -purpose) enters the path, and it makes a
# security property incidental rather than intentional.
#
# 🔴 spec.usages REPLACES the defaults, it does not merge. A leaf listing only "server auth" and
# "client auth" gets an EKU extension and NO Key Usage extension at all. Every leaf must list all
# four. The exact block is in the PR body and the runbook.
#
# ── why the CA certificates below deliberately set NO usages ─────────────────────────────────
#
# EKU is validated across the WHOLE chain, so a CA carrying an EKU list constrains every leaf
# beneath it — put "server auth" on the CA and client authentication breaks for all its leaves.
# cert-manager's own source says as much beside the defaults it removed: "it leads to strange
# behaviour when a user sets isCA: true (in which case, 'serverAuth' on the CA can break a lot of
# clients)". isCA already ORs in `cert sign` regardless of this field.
#
# ── rotationPolicy ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Set to Always explicitly. On v1.16.1 the effective default is Never — applied in controller
# code, not as a CRD schema default, which is why the installed CRD shows the enum with no
# default. Never means renewal REUSES the existing private key, so a leaked key stays valid across
# every future renewal and "rotation" never rotates the thing worth rotating. Setting it also
# means these manifests behave identically after an upgrade to v1.18+, where the default flips.
#
#
# Rendered only when parameters.common.internal_ca.enabled is true, so merging this file changes
# nothing until an environment opts in.
%{ for env in try(parameters.common.internal_ca.environments, []) ~}
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata:
  name: ${try(parameters.common.internal_ca.name_prefix, "fitmate-mtls")}-${env}-selfsigned
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: terraform
    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: internal-mtls
    fitmate.me/environment: ${env}
spec:
  # Bootstrap only. Signs exactly one thing — the ${env} root Certificate below — and exists
  # solely because selfSigned is the only issuer type that needs no pre-existing Secret.
  selfSigned: {}
---
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: Certificate
metadata:
  name: ${try(parameters.common.internal_ca.name_prefix, "fitmate-mtls")}-${env}
  # 🔴 MUST be cert-manager's --cluster-resource-namespace, NOT an arbitrary namespace: the `ca`
  # ClusterIssuer below resolves its secretName from there and nowhere else. This chart deploys
  # INTO cert-manager, so the chart namespace is the right value and stays correct if the release
  # is renamed. Override only if --cluster-resource-namespace is set to a namespace other than
  # the one cert-manager runs in.
  namespace: ${try(parameters.common.internal_ca.cluster_resource_namespace, namespace)}
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: terraform
    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: internal-mtls
    fitmate.me/environment: ${env}
spec:
  isCA: true
  # Self-signed root for THIS environment only. Being self-signed is what makes it the highest
  # certificate in every chain beneath it — which is exactly what makes a leaf's ca.crt resolve to
  # this CA rather than to something shared with another environment.
  commonName: ${try(parameters.common.internal_ca.name_prefix, "fitmate-mtls")}-${env}
  # The environment is in the SUBJECT, so `openssl x509 -issuer` on any leaf says out loud which
  # environment issued it. A cross-environment certificate is then obvious during triage instead
  # of something to reason out from namespace names.
  subject:
    organizations:
      - FITMate
    organizationalUnits:
      - ${env}
  secretName: ${try(parameters.common.internal_ca.name_prefix, "fitmate-mtls")}-${env}
  duration: ${try(parameters.common.internal_ca.ca_duration, "87600h")}
  renewBefore: ${try(parameters.common.internal_ca.ca_renew_before, "720h")}
  privateKey:
    algorithm: RSA
    size: 4096
    rotationPolicy: Always
  issuerRef:
    name: ${try(parameters.common.internal_ca.name_prefix, "fitmate-mtls")}-${env}-selfsigned
    kind: ClusterIssuer
    group: cert-manager.io
---
apiVersion: cert-manager.io/v1
kind: ClusterIssuer
metadata:
  name: ${try(parameters.common.internal_ca.name_prefix, "fitmate-mtls")}-${env}
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: terraform
    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: internal-mtls
    fitmate.me/environment: ${env}
spec:
  # ← THIS is the issuer a fitmate-<service>-${env} Certificate references:
  #     issuerRef: { name: <prefix>-${env}, kind: ClusterIssuer, group: cert-manager.io }
  ca:
    secretName: ${try(parameters.common.internal_ca.name_prefix, "fitmate-mtls")}-${env}
---
%{ endfor ~}
%{ endif ~}
